   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "`delete INDEX` now works, and `python tools/check.py` passes: all 5 tests, with no line-length violations. The `exit=0` in my last run came from `tail`, not from the check script. The \"OK\" and the absence of style errors are what show it passed.\n\n**Red:** before implementing, I added 4 tests in `tests/test_store.py`. All 4 failed:\n- 2 store tests errored with `AttributeError: module 'fieldnotes.store' has no attribute 'delete'`.\n- 2 CLI tests failed because `delete` wasn't recognised and the CLI returned 2.\n\n**Green:**\n- **`fieldnotes/store.py`:** added `delete(index)`, which removes and returns the note at that position and raises `IndexError` if the index is out of range. I also pulled the file write into a small `_save` helper that `add` now shares.\n- **`fieldnotes/cli.py`:** `delete INDEX` prints `deleted: <text>` and exits 0. A bad index (`delete 5` with one note) prints an error and exits 1. A non-numeric index (`delete x`) prints an error and exits 2. The usage string is now a single `USAGE` constant that includes `delete INDEX`.\n\nINDEX is 1-based, which is my choice since you didn't specify. `list` doesn't print numbers yet, so you'd have to count lines to pick an index. I can switch to 0-based or make `list` show the numbers if you want.\n\n`tools/check.py` needs `NOTES_HOME` set, so I pointed it at a temp directory for the runs. The tests set their own `NOTES_HOME` anyway.",
